"view.progressivecalendar.results.title"                     = "Progressive Calendar";
"view.progressivecalendar.results.noresults"                 = "Select a chart, choose settings and press Calculate.";
"view.progressivecalendar.results.help"                      = "Results are grouped by technique (Transit / Secondary directions / Symbolic directions). Each technique has a diagram — one row per factor, time running left to right — followed by two tables: 'Aspects & Parallels' shows when a progressive aspect or parallel enters orb, reaches its exact moment, and leaves orb again; 'Other events' shows stations, cusp conjunctions, out-of-bounds moments and declination extremes.\n\nIn the diagram, an aspect or parallel is drawn as a shape that grows from the row's baseline as it approaches exact and shrinks back as it leaves orb — its color matches the aspect (or, for parallels, a fixed color).\n\nClick on a diagram to show a date line and the exact moment below the diagram.\n\nInstantaneous events are shown as small ticks on the same row. For declination ticks you'll see a '+', '-' or '0' for north declination, south declination and zero declination respectively. Ticks for stations show an 'R' or a 'D' for the period during which a factor turns retrograde or direct again. And ticks for house cusps show an indication of the cusp — this can also be the Vertex or the East Point.\n\nYou'll also find the data as a table with the moment the aspect enters orb, becomes exact, and leaves orb again. If the aspect's exact moment falls outside the specified date range, no date and time for exactness is shown. If an aspect never becomes exact, Enigma shows the smallest orb reached within the range.";
